# TRIVAL project conventions for Cursor AI

## Project overview
TRIVAL is a two-stage computational literature review framework.
- Stage 1: BERTopic (BAAI/bge-small-en + UMAP + HDBSCAN) with Claude-based topic naming.
- Stage 2: Multi-LLM ensemble validation (BART + SBERT + Gemini 2.5 Flash).

## Code conventions
- Python 3.11+
- Type hints on all public functions.
- Docstrings in Google style for all public functions and classes.
- Prefer pure functions where possible; keep side effects (I/O, API calls) at module boundaries.
- All hyperparameters and model IDs live in `src/trival/config.py` — never hardcode in business logic.
- All API keys and file paths come from env vars via `src/trival/auth.py` and config.

## File organization
- Pipeline modules go in `src/trival/`.
- Classifier implementations go in `src/trival/classifiers/`.
- CLI entry points go in `scripts/`.
- One-off experiments stay in `notebooks/` (not `src/`).

## Testing
- Tests in `tests/` mirror the `src/trival/` structure.
- Use pytest fixtures for sample corpora.
- Mock all external API calls (Anthropic, Gemini) in unit tests; integration tests go behind a `@pytest.mark.integration` marker.

## Dependencies
- Prefer standard library when possible.
- Pin major versions in `requirements.txt`.
- Do not introduce new ML frameworks (keras, lightgbm, etc.) without discussion.

## Model usage
- Default Claude model for Stage 1: `claude-sonnet-5-5`. Configurable to Opus or Fable via `TRIVAL_CLAUDE_MODEL` env var.
- Default Gemini model for Stage 2: `gemini-2.5-flash`.
- Local models (BART, SBERT) load on first use; expect a cold-start download.

## Writing style for commit messages
Imperative mood: "add X", "fix Y", "refactor Z". Keep subject under 72 chars.
Multi-line body for the "why" when the diff doesn't make it obvious.
